{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "כי תצא", :translit "ki tétzé", :gloss "when you go out"} {:he "פרשת עגלה ערופה", :translit "parashat eglah arufah", :gloss "the portion of the decapitated heifer"} {:he "בין שתי מלחמות", :translit "bein shté milchamot", :gloss "between two wars"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Literary placement: the parashah of the eglah arufah (Deut 21:1-9) is flanked by war passages — before it the war with Midian (Num 31) and after it the war of ki tetzé (Deut 21:10)."}], :frame {:text "The mitzvah of the eglah arufah applies when a murdered corpse is found and the killer is unknown (Deuteronomy 21:1-9). Ba'al HaTurim observes that Scripture sandwiches this law between two war contexts, yielding the practical implications he derives. The rule that enemies were not sent to battle together reflects a concern for battlefield fraternicide.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 21:1-9" "Deuteronomy 21:10" "Sotah 44b" "Sifrei Devarim 192"]}} {:quotes [{:he "כי תעשה הישר", :translit "ki ta'aseh ha-yashar", :gloss "that you do what is right"} {:he "כי תצא למלחמה", :translit "ki tétzé la-milchamah", :gloss "when you go out to war"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut: the phrase 'that you do what is right' (Deut 12:28) is juxtaposed with 'when you go out to war' (Deut 21:10) to infer that only the righteous go to war."}], :frame {:text "The Talmud states that only the righteous go out to the voluntary war (milchemet reshut). Sifrei links doing what is right in God's eyes as a precondition for military success. The draft selects the tzadik among those drawn by lot, as Rabbi Yosi ha-Galili teaches that only the tzadik goes forth.", :sources ["Sotah 44a" "Sifrei Devarim 192" "Deuteronomy 12:28" "Deuteronomy 21:10"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ה'", :translit "HaShem", :gloss "the LORD"} {:he "כי תצא", :translit "ki tétzé", :gloss "when you go out"} {:he "ל.מ.לחמה", :translit "l.m.lachamah", :gloss "to war"} {:he "ויצא ה' ונלחם בגוים", :translit "ve-yatzá HaShem ve-nilcham ba-goyim", :gloss "and HaShem will go out and fight against the nations"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut: the divine name at the end of the prior verse (Deut 21:9, mentioning HaShem) is juxtaposed with 'when you go out' (Deut 21:10), signaling that HaShem Himself goes out to war alongside Israel."}], :frame {:text "The verse 'HaShem will go out and fight against the nations' (Zechariah 14:3) depicts God as a warrior who accompanies Israel's armies into battle. Deuteronomy 21:9 ends with the phrase 'that you may prosper,' mentioning HaShem, immediately before the war passage of 21:10.", :sources ["Zechariah 14:3" "Deuteronomy 21:9-10"]}} {:quotes [{:he "כי תצא", :translit "ki tétzé", :gloss "when you go out"} {:he "צא אליהם ואל תניחם לבא עליך", :translit "tzé aleihem ve-al tanicham la-vo alecha", :gloss "go out against them and do not let them come upon you"} {:he "פן ישחיתו הארץ", :translit "pen yashchitu ha-aretz", :gloss "lest they destroy the land"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Midrashic reading: the phrase 'when you go out' is read as 'go out against them' — offensive rather than passive, to preempt enemy devastation of the land."}], :frame {:text "The Sifrei on this verse states: 'Go out against them' — this teaches that Israel should take the battle to the enemy rather than wait to be invaded. The Midrash Tanhuma explains that delay invites the enemy to destroy the land.", :sources ["Sifrei Devarim 192" "Midrash Tanhuma, Ki Tetzé 1" "Deuteronomy 21:10"]}} {:quotes [{:he "על אויבך", :translit "al oyvecha", :gloss "upon your enemy"} {:he "חסר יו\"ד", :translit "chaser yud", :gloss "defective (missing) yod"} {:he "שבמלחמת יחיד הכתוב מדבר", :translit "she-be-milchemet yachid ha-katuv medaber", :gloss "that the verse speaks of a war of the individual"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Masoretic observation: the word אויבך is written defectively (without a yod), signaling that the verse addresses a war of the individual rather than a national campaign."}], :frame {:text "The word אויבך appears without the letter yod in this verse, a spelling noted in standard Masoretic references as a defective form. Ba'al HaTurim reads this deficiency as a signal that the verse deals with an individual battle rather than communal war.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 21:10" "Minchat Shai on Deuteronomy 21:10"]}} {:quotes [{:he "שביו", :translit "shivyó", :gloss "its captivity"} {:he "ואם יהיו כנענים", :translit "ve-im yihyu kena'anim", :gloss "and if they are Canaanites"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "שביו", :b "ואם יהיו כנענים"}], :frame {:text "The gematria equating 'its captivity' with 'and if they are Canaanites' signals that the female captive of Deuteronomy 21:10-14 may be a Canaanite woman. This aligns with the halakhic category of the beautiful captive (yefat to'ar) taken in battle. Rashi and Sifrei address the moral danger of taking such a captive and the progressive safeguards the Torah prescribes.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 21:10-14" "Sifrei Devarim 211" "Rashi on Deuteronomy 21:11" "Kiddushin 21b-22a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Literary placement: the parashah of the eglah arufah (Deut 21:1-9) is flanked by war passages — before it the war with Midian (Num 31) and after it the war of ki tetzé (Deut 21:10).", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Semikhut: the phrase 'that you do what is right' (Deut 12:28) is juxtaposed with 'when you go out to war' (Deut 21:10) to infer that only the righteous go to war.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Semikhut: the divine name at the end of the prior verse (Deut 21:9, mentioning HaShem) is juxtaposed with 'when you go out' (Deut 21:10), signaling that HaShem Himself goes out to war alongside Israel.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Midrashic reading: the phrase 'when you go out' is read as 'go out against them' — offensive rather than passive, to preempt enemy devastation of the land.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Masoretic observation: the word אויבך is written defectively (without a yod), signaling that the verse addresses a war of the individual rather than a national campaign.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "שביו", :a-value 318, :b "ואם יהיו כנענים", :b-value 318, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:37:59.733454070Z", :model "glm-5.2"}